  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved governance</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australia&#x2019;s green growth investment in PNG supported government organisations at the national and subnational level become more accountable under PNG&#x2019;s international climate commitments, and develop inclusive green growth policies and mechanisms. The Climate Change Development Authority (CCDA) is the lead government agency on climate change adaptation and mitigation. Both the Climate Resilient Green Growth and Enabling Green Growth projects supported CCDA to improve its processes for mandatory climate reporting under UNFCCC, and to make necessary amendments to the Climate Change Management Act. At the provincial level, CRGG supported and provided technical assistance to Enga, Milne Bay and New Ireland provincial governments to develop and adopt Green Growth Action Plans and establish provincial climate change offices and committees. These provinces, through the strengthened and newly established organisations, are now able to implement inclusive green growth policies at the community level.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Climate adaptation, mitigation, environment and biodiversity</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Enabling Green Growth (EGG) is a project that advanced PNG&#x2019;s REDD+ readiness. The project strengthened the enabling environment for accessing REDD+ climate financing to address climate change and conserve biodiversity in Papua New Guinea. Support in this project included the preparation and publication of four REDD+ readiness guidelines - National REDD+ Free Prior and Informed Consent Guidelines, National REDD+ Development Guidelines, National REDD+ Benefit Sharing and Distribution Guidelines, and National Grievance Redress Mechanism Guidelines.  Technical support to improve the National Forest Monitoring and Reporting System resulted in the upgrading of satellite imaging with the latest technology for forest monitoring systems. As a result, Papua New Guineans can now have real-time and live interaction on forest data from the CCDA Forest portal.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Climate adaptation, mitigation, environment and biodiversity</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Training and Capacity Building (TrainCap) built capacity for PNG Government staff and institutions, particularly CCDA staff, to effectively implement the PNG national climate change policy and source climate finance opportunities.
&#x200d;
Short training courses on climate resilience, climate finance and climate resilience project design were provided to 203 key stakeholders from national and subnational government organisations and the private sector. As a result of this capacity building support, these stakeholders are now able to implement climate policies at both the government and community level.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
